# Experience with design and product
As part of my most recent co-op, I worked closely with my team's UI/UX designers and product owner to introduce a new major feature to my team's product. The new feature required a complete overhaul of the existing application infrastructure and design resources, and so maintaining constant communication with both the PO and the designers was incredibly important. Having a bit of design experience greatly helped me too, as I was able to more concisely convey opinions in domain-specific language that minimized confusion in my suggestions.

# Best trait as developer
I feel my best trait as a developer is my ability to approach new problems in code, design, or architecture, quickly absorb background information and requirements, and then work out a plan forward using my skillset and newly gained knowledge. 

It's incredibly helpful for when I get thrown into codebases that I'm unfamiliar with, as I can get the "lay of the land" fairly quickly, and then dedicate my time to learning the necessary skills that I don't yet have to effectively contribute to the task. 

# What I'm looking for 
I'm looking for a few things, frankly. 

Firstly, I want to further improve my development skills - writing good code is like exercising a muscle. Every chance I get to reach and exceed the limits of my knowledge is an opportunity to come out of it with a new skill or more knowledge. 

Secondly, I want to experience a wide range of different company cultures. My first co-op was at a small company, and my second two were an extremely large company. I find that part of the co-op experience is learning where you work and fit in the best, and applying to interesting companies, such as 1Password, is the perfect way to do that.

Finally, I find the XAM platform interesting! As a person who has interacted with a myriad of different IAM systems, being able to have a direct hand in developing a next-generation, extended variation of IAM excites me!